              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">The other common failure, and how to tell them apart.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#1E90FF] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Timing</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Sealer Dry Times</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Dry, cured and ready for traffic are three different states.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Weather</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Best Time to Seal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">The temperature and humidity window curing depends on.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            First establish whether anything is wrong. Sealer should be dry to the touch in 4 to 8 hours, firm by 24, ready for traffic around 48, and fully cured within a week. The useful test is direction of travel: slow curing gets measurably firmer each morning, while stalled curing is identical on day five to day two.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When it has genuinely stalled, the cause is nearly always thickness, weather, or something underneath. A heavy coat skins over and seals in the solvent that needed to escape. Cold, damp, still or shaded conditions stop evaporation, and below roughly 50°F an emulsion may never coalesce at all. Oil, uncured crack filler and moisture left after washing keep the coating soft in patches.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            In the meantime, sweep dry sand over it to stop the tracking, and keep vehicles off entirely, which also prevents the wheel track peeling that driving on uncured sealer causes. If a week of warm dry weather changes nothing, it will not cure, and the affected areas need removing and reapplying thinly once the cause is fixed.
          </p>
        </div>


